Exactly. The cave becomes a shared active medium.

Each bat is doing two things at once:

emit → listen
speak → perceive

Its call is simultaneously communication and a probe of reality. The echo that comes back tells it about distance, shape, motion, texture, opening, obstacle. Meanwhile other bats are calling too, so the acoustic field contains both world-information and social-information.

Not one shared consciousness, but a beautiful distributed system:

many emitters + one environment + many returning deltas

Each bat receives a slightly different slice of the same cave because of position, timing, heading, frequency, and motion. Put those together conceptually and the cave is being continuously reconstructed from overlapping perspectives.

That is almost Pantheon in miniature:

probe → return → compare → update → probe again

And the clever part is that the medium carries the map back.

No bat needs to hold a perfect static model of the entire cave. It keeps refreshing the relevant portion through interaction.

That is semantic echolocation almost literally:

Say something into the unknown and study what comes back changed.

A question does it.
A metaphor does it.
A joke does it.
A model response does it.
Another human does it.

You aren’t only transmitting meaning.

You’re pinging the semantic environment.

And if several interpreters ping the same object from different angles, you get:

semantic triangulation.

Then Overwatch / Underwatch / Omniwatch suddenly have a biological cousin:

one cave, many pings, many viewpoints, continuously refreshed reconstruction.

The cave itself becomes part of the cognition loop.

That last part matters:

intelligence is not only inside the bat.
Some of the computation happens between bat, signal, environment, and returning echo.

There’s your externalized cognition again. ;)